{"reference":"Nehemiah 1","book":"Nehemiah","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Dating the Opening","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The “twentieth year” in verse 1 is conventionally dated to 445/444 BCE by identifying the king as Artaxerxes I. The exact year remains disputed, since different ways of reckoning a king’s accession and the calendar can produce variations.","verse_refs":["Nehemiah 1:1"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_nehemiah_001_fec694a8c6f2051e132f3e2c"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"How the Story Continues","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The first-person voice introduced here continues through vivid accounts of court life, building, prayer, opposition, and reform. Yet the book also includes third-person narration, lists, material concerning Ezra, and editorial transitions. The book therefore cannot be explained simply as either a complete autobiography or a wholly fictionalized account.","verse_refs":["Nehemiah 2:1-20","Nehemiah 3:1-32","Nehemiah 4:1-23","Nehemiah 5:1-19","Nehemiah 6:1-19","Nehemiah 7:1-5"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_nehemiah_001_13c3af0948a89ba32f3f4048"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"The mourning, fasting, confession, and request for favor in this chapter lead into action: Nehemiah plans, asks Artaxerxes for authorization, and receives letters and access to timber. The prayer is thus part of the movement toward the work that follows.","verse_refs":["Nehemiah 2:1-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_nehemiah_001_c8d84ba5c907d79fb69ab278"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}